@@ -116,7 +116,7 @@ and product-specific color contrast thresholds remain distinct until their contr
 | Noninteractive GUI-test activation blocking | `RedSalamander::TestSupport::ScopedWindowActivationBlocker` in `Common/Common.Tests.WindowActivationGuard.h` | GUI test executables and in-product selftests whose contract excludes real focus must hold this UI-thread CBT guard across window creation and execution. It adds `WS_EX_NOACTIVATE` to top-level test windows and rejects attempted activation/focus transfers. Tests that genuinely validate foreground, caret, menu, pointer, or keyboard routing must run without the guard and retain their interactive-desktop plan marker. |
 | Cooperative application foreground requests | `Common::RequestForegroundWindow` in `Common/WindowActivation.h` | Application production callers use this wrapper. In a test-enabled build it declines the request before entering User32 when the owning UI thread holds the no-activation guard. Outside that scope, including ordinary production builds and real-input tests, it forwards the original HWND and Win32 BOOL result unchanged. The CBT guard remains a fallback and foreground observation remains independent; this helper does not alter logical control focus or native text-input setup. |
 | Nonactivating desktop observations | `DesktopSnapshotSource`, `DesktopForegroundEvents`, `EvaluateDesktopObservation` and `ScopedNonActivatingDesktopObserver` in `Tests/TestSupport/TestSupport.Tests.DesktopObservation.h` | Read-only UIA-test evidence behind the existing activation guard. Process-filtered WinEvents retain transient owned foreground; a private message-only window's name-change notification drains the ordered callback queue within 250 ms. Guard/capture/drain failures and owned foreground fail closed. Foreign foreground, cursor and last-input changes are diagnostic counts, never grounds to restore desktop state or waive owned activation. Snapshot injection supplies deterministic interference without physical input. |
-| Contained child processes | `RunChildProcess` in `Tests/TestSupport/TestSupport.Tests.ChildProcess.h` | Reuse structured arguments, concurrent bounded output drains, timeout handling, and kill-on-close Job containment. Optional nonempty `environmentOverrides` builds one child-only Unicode environment, preserving inherited drive pseudo-variables, resolving inherited case aliases through Windows lookup, sorting keys ordinally without case, rejecting malformed/duplicate explicit overrides, and retaining the double-NUL block through CreateProcess. Empty overrides preserve ambient inheritance. Never mutate the pumping parent process environment to configure an owned child. |
+| Contained child processes | `RunChildProcess` in `Tests/TestSupport/TestSupport.Tests.ChildProcess.h` | Reuse structured arguments, concurrent bounded output drains, timeout handling, and kill-on-close Job containment. Console children launch suspended with `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`, receive their restricted standard handles, and resume only after Job assignment; they neither allocate an interactive console nor inherit the parent's console-control lifetime. GUI children retain their normal window behavior. Optional nonempty `environmentOverrides` builds one child-only Unicode environment, preserving inherited drive pseudo-variables, resolving inherited case aliases through Windows lookup, sorting keys ordinally without case, rejecting malformed/duplicate explicit overrides, and retaining the double-NUL block through CreateProcess. Empty overrides preserve ambient inheritance. Never mutate the pumping parent process environment to configure an owned child. |
 | Test failure-report routing | `RedSalamander::TestSupport::RouteFailureReportsAwayFromDialogs` in `Tests/TestSupport/TestSupport.Tests.FailureReports.h`; `Common::DebugSelfTest::Check` in `Common/Helpers.h` | Call the report router first in every native test entry point, and on the self-test paths of the test-enabled product executables, so a failed Debug-CRT or STL check ends the run with its report on stderr and exit code 3 instead of a modal dialog. Plugin assertions reuse `Check`, which preserves the exact failed message on stderr. Its optional failure reporter lets the FTP fixture also capture `SelfTest.CheckFailure` in the existing JSONL stream when a GUI host has no inherited console, without imposing a metrics-library dependency on standalone plugins. Do not install a second report hook; `Specs/Testing/Testing_SelfTests.md` owns the contract. |
 | Guarded tooling output paths and file publication | `Resolve-RSGuardedRepositoryPath` and `Publish-RSGuardedRepositoryFile` in `Tools/Modules/Build/ArtifactOperationLock.psm1` | Normalize caller-selected tooling destinations beneath one exact repository-relative root, reject every existing reparse component, create missing directories segment-by-segment when requested, and revalidate after creation. Final file publication uses a validated same-directory staged file and atomic move/replace so a failed publication preserves the prior complete destination. Packaging callers must use these helpers instead of lexical prefix checks or force-overwrite commands. |
 | Directed desktop-input warning | `Tests/TestSupport/TestSupport.Tests.DirectedSelfTestInputWarning.h` | Reuses the original Commands warning across Commands, DxUi Menu/NativeTextInput and interactive ViewerPE/ViewerSqlite cases. A suite guard owns one large, DPI-scaled, centered, non-activating, click-through surface with a three-second lead; nested input guards borrow it. No-activation lanes and case listing remain silent. The warning and runner desktop mutex do not themselves restore focus or cursor: the input owner must implement and verify its cleanup boundary. `TryActivateOwnedWindow` requires a visible shared warning and a process/UI-thread-owned target, refuses an active no-activation guard, and falls back to a bounded registered-hotkey permission handshake without joining foreign input queues. Its key-down remains held through the real hotkey handler's activation attempt; every exit releases the key and retires its registration. Release failure rejects admission and retains scoped cleanup. Commands case isolation and the opt-in FileOps interaction gallery reuse it and require actual owned foreground after the handoff. |
diff --git a/Specs/Testing/Testing_SelfTests.md b/Specs/Testing/Testing_SelfTests.md
index b1df8cca0..44cc14c0b 100644
--- a/Specs/Testing/Testing_SelfTests.md
+++ b/Specs/Testing/Testing_SelfTests.md
@@ -515,6 +515,7 @@ Environment variables may select alternate test inputs such as profile names, bu
 - Recycled test items are purged by the case that created them. A Recycle moves a test file into the user's `<volume>\$Recycle.Bin\<SID>`, outside every marked sandbox, so the Local provider's test build (`ENABLE_TESTS`) journals each Recycle Bin item its `IFileOperation` progress sinks see created (`PostDeleteItem` `newlyCreated`) for an original inside a marked `X:\RedSalamander.Perf`: the original path and the `$R` entry's file-system path, bounded, taken by the host through `RedSalamanderFileSystemTestTakeRecycledItems`. Recycles of any other path are not recorded. Every case boundary takes the journal and purges exactly those items, whatever the case's outcome: `SelfTest::RunCaseAttempt` after each Commands or Compare case body, and the FileOperations `RecordCurrentPhase` at every phase end, failed and skipped phases included. A Recycle still finishing when a suite's last case returned is caught by `SelfTest::PurgeRecycledTestItemsAtSuiteEnd`, which the Commands suite calls after the main window's file operations drain and the Compare suite calls after its last case; items it cannot purge fail the suite through a `recycle_bin_hygiene` result. `SelfTest::PurgeRecycledTestItem` deletes an item only after proving that the `$R` entry lies directly inside the current user's `$Recycle.Bin\<SID>` on the original's volume with reparse-free ancestors, and that the matching `$I` record (version 1 or 2) names that exact original, which must be a strict descendant of a marked sandbox and of this run (the self-test root, or `runs\<runId>` under the unified root or under the marked base on the original's volume, or the ad-hoc run root); it then removes the `$R` entry without following links and the `$I` record, and checks that neither remains. It never empties the Recycle Bin and never enumerates or touches any other item. An item it cannot prove, or fails to remove, and a journal that dropped records, fail that case. `Phase7_RecycleBinTestItemsPurged` proves the journal names exactly the recycled file and directory, that forged records (an original outside every sandbox, an entry outside `$Recycle.Bin`, a `$I` record naming another original) are refused without deleting anything, and that the purge leaves none of the case's items in the Recycle Bin. `Phase7_RecycleBinBatchDelete` (both the single-item and the batch progress sink) and `Phase7_RecycleBinBatchDeleteMultiBatch` require the purge to find exactly as many journaled items as the task recycled, so a sink that stops journaling fails them.
 - FileOperations queue-state selftests must prove the queue state they assert. Tests that cancel a queued task must start the active holder first, wait until that task has entered operation and owns the active slot, then start the queued task, assert `IsWaitingInQueue()` before queued-cancel assertions, and fail fast if the queued task enters operation or completes before cancellation. Timeout diagnostics must include task ids, live/completed state, queue flags, progress, and HRESULTs for every task participating in the queue proof.
 - Compare selftests that launch `RedSalamanderSearchService.exe --run-foreground` must create a JobObject with `JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE`, start the child process suspended with `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`, assign it with `AssignProcessToJobObject`, and resume the main thread only after assignment succeeds. The selftest-owned child must not share the broad runner's console-control lifetime, and it must not survive a selftest harness crash or timeout and hold SQLite/WAL or scratch-root handles into the next run.
+- The canonical captured-child helper enforces the same console isolation. `test_support_child_process_runner_contract` observes the service probe's actual `GetConsoleWindow` state in addition to its existing stream, quoting, exit-code, restricted-inheritance, timeout-tree and cancellation controls. Duplicate foreground service checks preserve the child's output and trace its exact exit code and the primary writer's lifetime; a passing isolated retry alone does not establish the cause of a broad-run failure.
 - Compare selftests that gate foreground SearchService readiness must poll the explicit service status for the intended pipe and must diagnose early child-process exit with exit code plus captured stdout/stderr. Query-performance and parity tests must establish service readiness before the first warmup query; the first query is not an acceptable readiness probe.
 - Foreground SearchService readiness polling uses a scaled, bounded 30-second default. Freshly linked images and cold SQLite stores can legitimately spend more than 10 seconds in image verification and initialization before creating their unique pipe; successful warm starts still return immediately, and cases with a tighter semantic budget may pass an explicit override.
 - Foreground SearchService fault-injection and first-query gates must use the process-aware status helper with child output capture. A timeout, broken pipe, or early exit must retain the child exit code and captured stdout/stderr instead of reporting only the client-side pipe HRESULT.
